<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Costa Rica</h1><p class="st-meta">1848 CE – present · capital San José</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central America's most stable democracy, independent since 1848, known for coffee, abolishing its army in 1948 and its long peaceful elections.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Having left the Central American federation in 1838, Costa Rica formally declared itself a republic on 31 August 1848 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-castro-madriz">José María Castro Madriz</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> exports through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntarenas">Puntarenas</a> had already created a prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-valley-costa-rica">Central Valley</a> elite and a large class of smallholders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-rafael-mora-porras">Juan Rafael Mora Porras</a> led the Central American war that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> in 1856 to 1857, in which the drummer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-santamaria">Juan Santamaría</a> became a national hero. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-guardia-gutierrez">Tomás Guardia</a>'s rule from 1870 began the railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-limon">Limón</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minor-c-keith">Minor Keith</a>, which spawned the banana industry. The death penalty was abolished in 1882, and the 1889 election is often regarded as the first truly free one. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-tinoco-granados">Tinoco</a> dictatorship of 1917 to 1919 was a rare break.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After a short civil war in 1948, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-figueres-ferrer">José Figueres</a> abolished the army, and the 1949 constitution confirmed it. Costa Rica has since held regular peaceful elections, became a leader in ecotourism and conservation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-arias">Óscar Arias</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1987 for Central American peace efforts.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-costa-rica.jpg" alt="Republic of Costa Rica" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Costa_Rica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1848 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San José</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Rafael Mora Porras, José Figueres Ferrer, Óscar Arias</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Costa Rica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1937&amp;focus=republic-of-costa-rica" aria-label="Republic of Costa Rica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2361 1807 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Costa Rica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2681 2063L2681 2063"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2681 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2681 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2681 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San José. <a href="/atlas?year=1937&amp;focus=republic-of-costa-rica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
